@startuml 03-adjudication-sequence
' How one batch is adjudicated, including the version-arbitration ordering.
' Render: make diagrams

title Adjudication of one batch — why the phase order matters

skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam sequenceArrowColor #445566
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #445566
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFF8E7
skinparam NoteBorderColor #C8A96A

participant "QUEUE" as Q
participant "int_trade_event_typed" as T
participant "reference seeds\ncp / cur / p / bk" as REF
participant "FIELD rules" as F
participant "version\narbitration" as ARB
participant "STATE rules" as S
participant "FCT_TRADE\n(prior state)" as PRIOR
database "destinations" as D

Q -> T : raw VARIANT payload

group Step 1 — typing
  T -> T : try_to_date / try_to_number / ...
  T -> T : count casts that failed
  note right of T
    A try_ cast returns NULL instead of raising,
    so one bad field cannot abort the batch.
    But NULL-from-failed-cast then looks identical
    to never-sent — hence cast_failure_count.

    RJ004 = "you did not send it"
    RJ008 = "you sent it unreadable"
    Different teams, different fixes.
  end note
end

group Step 2 — FIELD phase (14 rules)
  T -> F : typed event
  REF -> F : left joins on counterparty,\ncurrency (x2), product, book
  F -> F : evaluate **every** rule, no short-circuit
  note right of F
    array_construct_compact collects every code
    that fired. A capture team is told all four
    of its problems at once, instead of learning
    one per resubmission.
  end note
end

group Step 3 — version arbitration
  F -> ARB : **field-valid events only**
  PRIOR -> ARB : stored version, is_cancelled
  ARB -> ARB : effective_prior_version =\ngreatest(stored, highest accepted earlier in run)
  ARB -> ARB : intra_run_rank by arrival order
  note right of ARB #FBEAEA
    **The critical filter.**

    If a malformed v5 participated here, it would
    set the mark to 5 — and a valid v3 arriving
    next would be rejected as stale, on the
    authority of an event we already threw away.

    One bad message would poison the trade's
    entire subsequent history, and every
    resulting rejection would look correct.
  end note
end

group Step 4 — STATE phase (3 rules)
  ARB -> S : arbitration columns
  S -> S : RJ001 stale version\nRJ009 superseded in run\nRJ010 amend after cancel
end

S -> D : verdict + violated_rule_codes

alt no REJECT-severity code fired
  D -> D : **ACCEPTED**\n-> FCT_TRADE + FCT_TRADE_VERSION
  note right of D
    WARN codes (RJ018 limit breach,
    RJ019 duplicate UTI) are recorded
    against an *accepted* trade.
    Rejecting a limit breach would just
    push it into three smaller tickets.
  end note
else RJ009 only
  D -> D : **SUPERSEDED**\n-> FCT_TRADE_REJECTED
  note right of D
    Excluded from the reject-rate gate:
    it is R2 working correctly, and counting
    it would fire the gate on healthy
    amendment traffic.
  end note
else any REJECT code
  D -> D : **REJECTED**\n-> FCT_TRADE_REJECTED **+ raw_payload**
end

D -> D : every rule hit -> TRADE_RULE_RESULT

@enduml
